---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { meta } from "../data/pages";
import { installCommand } from "../data/install";
---
<Layout {...meta("/quickstart/")}>
  <article class="frame prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Quickstart · local machine</p>
    <h1 class="display">Your first session through Torana.</h1>
    <p>Which coding harness do you already use? Start with that. You need Git and Go 1.26.6 or newer to build Torana; a separate provider API key is only needed for the direct API-key path below. Your provider’s normal usage limits and billing still apply.</p>
    <p>Run Torana locally, keep the listener on loopback, and review each plugin’s requested capabilities before enabling it.</p>
    <p>Using a local model server? You can connect it too. If its API differs from your harness’s, use an <a href="/docs/protocol-bridges/">API bridge</a>.</p>
    <h2>1. Install</h2>
    <pre><code>{installCommand}</code></pre>
    <p>This walkthrough builds from source. To reproduce a particular revision, check out its reviewed commit SHA before building.</p>
    <h2>2. Start without plugins</h2>
    <pre><code>{`export TORANA_DATA_DIR="$PWD/.torana-data"
./torana --debug start
./torana status`}</code></pre>
    <p>This keeps evaluation state in <code>.torana-data</code> inside the checkout. On first run Torana imports <code>config.json</code>; later changes belong in the local control plane, not the seed file. This directory can contain credentials and plugin files: keep it private. Open <code>http://127.0.0.1:8080/_torana/</code> to inspect activity or edit providers. The listener and control plane default to loopback. The server runs in the background. In the same shell, verify it:</p>
    <pre><code>curl --fail-with-body --silent http://127.0.0.1:8080/health</code></pre>
    <p>A healthy process returns <code>{`{"status":"ok"}`}</code>. A failed plugin reload keeps the last known-good pipeline serving but reports a degraded health status.</p>
    <h2>3. Which harness do you use?</h2>
    <p>Pick one route. You do not need to configure every provider.</p>
    <h3>Claude Code</h3>
    <p>Already signed in? Keep the example’s <code>anthropic</code> provider on <strong>Use harness credentials</strong>, then launch Claude Code with a command-scoped address:</p>
    <pre><code>ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080/provider/anthropic claude</code></pre>
    <p>Do not add a DeepSeek key or replace your login. If your shell already sets an Anthropic key/token or your Claude configuration uses a key helper, that credential may take precedence. The <a href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-edge/blob/main/docs/HARNESS_SETUP.md#claude-code">Claude Code guide</a> explains the distinction.</p>
    <details>
      <summary>Claude replies, but the Feed is empty?</summary>
      <p>Check <code>./torana status</code> and open that instance’s control plane. Exit Claude Code and start a fresh session with the command above, using your Torana instance’s port and provider name. A session launched normally will not inherit that command-scoped routing setting.</p>
      <p>Separately, to check for credentials that could override your Claude login, run this in the shell where you launch Claude. It prints variable names only, never their values. Also check whether your Claude configuration uses a key helper. Credential overrides affect authentication and billing; they do not by themselves mean that traffic bypasses Torana.</p>
      <pre><code>{`for name in ANTHROPIC_API_KEY ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN; do
  if printenv "$name" >/dev/null; then
    printf '%s is set\\n' "$name"
  fi
done`}</code></pre>
      <p>Do not paste tokens, a full environment dump, or credential-bearing configuration into an issue.</p>
    </details>
    <h3>Codex, Antigravity, pi, or oh-my-pi</h3>
    <p>Keep your usual harness and follow its recipe in the <a href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-edge/blob/main/docs/HARNESS_SETUP.md">setup guide</a>. Choose the Codex recipe for your ChatGPT login or API key; for Antigravity, the guide connects your existing Google login through Torana’s local TLS ingress.</p>
    <p>Using Codex with plugins? Follow the guide’s HTTP/SSE configuration (<code>supports_websockets = false</code>) so response hooks and usage logging run. For Antigravity, start interactively to approve tool actions, or follow its guide for a single headless task with explicit permissions.</p>
    <p>Ask your harness to read a small, non-sensitive file. Open the local control plane’s <strong>Feed</strong> to see the request, model, and status. Next, add the usage logger below and look for its new local record: that confirms the plugin ran, beyond getting a reply from the model.</p>
    <details id="api-key">
    <summary>Alternatively: use a provider API key directly</summary>
    <p>Have an API key and want to send a request without a harness? This example uses DeepSeek. For another provider, change the provider URL, format and authentication in Settings, then adapt the request endpoint, model and payload to that provider’s API.</p>
    <pre><code>{`export DEEPSEEK_API_KEY='replace-with-your-deepseek-key'

curl --fail-with-body --silent \\
  http://127.0.0.1:8080/provider/deepseek/v1/chat/completions \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $DEEPSEEK_API_KEY" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"model":"deepseek-flash","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Reply with exactly: Torana works"}]}'`}</code></pre>
    <p>This uses the example configuration’s DeepSeek route and forwards your key to DeepSeek. Choose a model available to your account. You do not need DeepSeek for the harness routes above.</p>
    </details>
    <pre><code>{`./torana feed
./torana stats`}</code></pre>
    <p>Find the matching request in the feed. You can also inspect it in the local UI. <code>./torana status</code> reports the instance and log path.</p>
    <h2>4. Add one useful plugin</h2>
    <p>From the same Torana checkout, install the usage logger:</p>
    <pre><code>./torana plugin install https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-plugins/tree/main/plugins/usage_logger</code></pre>
    <p>Open the local control plane, select <strong>usage_logger</strong>, review its requested file access and retention budget, then choose <strong>Approve and enable</strong>. Installation alone does not grant access. For terminal or agent automation instead, follow the <a href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-plugins/blob/main/plugins/usage_logger/README.md#configure">plugin’s CLI setup guide</a>.</p>
    <p>Send another request from the same harness, then read the result:</p>
    <pre><code>{`tail -F "$(./torana plugin file path usage_logger usage.jsonl)"`}</code></pre>
    <p>Each JSONL record includes provider, model, latency, status, and reported input/output/cache token counts. It does not contain prompts, responses, or headers. You now have a concrete check that requests are reaching Torana and the plugin is running.</p>
    <p>Next, <a href="/plugins/">explore the other plugins</a> or <a href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-plugin-sdk">write your own</a>.</p>
    <h2>Switch back whenever you want</h2>
    <p>Exit the harness and launch it normally. The command-scoped Claude Code example does not change your saved settings or later shell commands. If you edited a harness configuration file, restore its previous settings. Once your agent connects directly again, stop Torana with <code>./torana stop --yes</code>. Use <code>./torana serve</code> if you prefer foreground serving. The evaluation directory remains on disk until you remove it.</p>
    <h2>Help improve the next release</h2>
    <p><a href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-edge/issues">Tell us how your session went</a>: your Torana and harness versions, provider/endpoint, whether streaming and tool calls worked, and the first confusing step. Keep credentials, private source, and sensitive prompts out of your report.</p>
  </article>
</Layout>
